# slc10 tarballs live in alibuild-cas, NOT in alibuild-repo: they are published
# by the reapi/CAS pipeline, while alibuild-repo remains the legacy store that
# still serves slc9. TARS/slc10_x86-64/ in alibuild-repo is completely empty, so
# with the default store this check finds no hits and rebuilds the entire stack
# from source, starting with a 982 MB GCC-Toolchain.
#
# Consumable over plain HTTP, no credentials and no s3:// client needed. The
# layout has two hops: the package path is the classic symlink object whose body
# is the relative store/ path, and the store/ object is a ~78 byte stub carrying
#
#   x-amz-website-redirect-location: /cas/sha256/<xx>/<digest>
#
# HttpRemoteSync.getRetry reads that header and re-fetches the target, so the
# store resolves transparently. That is NOT 2.0-only: v1.17.43 -- the release
# pinned in repo-config/DEFAULTS.env -- and master both have it (sync.py, the
# `if redirect_base:` block, passed only on the tarball fetch). So this override
# is about WHERE the slc10 tarballs are, not about which aliBuild can read them.
#
# Note s3cmd does not follow the header at all; the publisher tells a stub from
# a tarball by content instead (gzip magic vs a short path). Anything fetching
# these objects directly, rather than through aliBuild, needs the same care.
# Reads the LEGACY tree from alibuild-repo, not alibuild-cas. Both buckets hold
# a complete slc10 tree today, but only alibuild-repo keeps getting one: since
# 2026-08-31 the release builder writes its legacy links there
# (--legacy-links-store in slc10-release/DEFAULTS.env), so anything published
# from now on is invisible to a check still reading alibuild-cas -- it would
# silently rebuild from source instead of reusing.
#
# The bytes have not moved. Objects under TARS/ are ~78-byte stubs in either
# bucket; the only difference is that alibuild-repo's redirect is ABSOLUTE
# (https://s3.cern.ch/swift/v1/alibuild-cas/cas/sha256/...) where alibuild-cas's
# is relative. HttpRemoteSync follows both, so this is the same number of
# requests per tarball and should be performance-neutral. If round durations
# move after 2026-08-31, that assumption is what to question first.
REMOTE_STORE=https://s3.cern.ch/swift/v1/alibuild-repo

# No ONLY_PRS: the check now takes the whole alidist queue. It was pinned to
# 6299 (Freetype) and 6193 (ONNXRuntime) for the bring-up; both went green on
# slc10, so the allowlist has done its job. Set it again -- a push, not a
# redeploy, since the *.env is re-fetched every round -- to re-narrow.

CI_NAME=build_O2_alidist_slc10_x86
PACKAGE=O2
ALIBUILD_DEFAULTS=o2
PR_REPO=alisw/alidist
PR_BRANCH=master
NO_ASSUME_CONSISTENT_EXTERNALS=
TRUST_COLLABORATORS=true
CHECK_NAME=build/O2/alidist-slc10-x86
DONT_USE_COMMENTS=1
DEVEL_PKGS="$PR_REPO $PR_BRANCH
AliceO2Group/AliceO2 dev O2"
# aliBuild 2.0, which is the builder slc10 is being brought up against -- so the
# check exercises the two together rather than validating slc10 on a builder
# nobody will use. Overriding here leaves every other check on the pinned
# version from repo-config/DEFAULTS.env.
#
# Two things make it necessary, and reading the store is not one of them:
#   - VALID_ARCHS_RE in v1.17.43 is "^slc[5-9]_...", a character class that
#     cannot express 10, so it rejects slc10_x86-64 outright;
#   - reapi:// stores, which the release builder writes to, arrived in the same
#     PR.
#
# A ref, not a tag or SHA: the loop reinstalls before every build, so the check
# follows the PR as it develops -- which is the point during bring-up, and also
# means a broken commit on the PR breaks the check rather than silently testing
# a stale build. Verified that pip resolves this form and that the branch still
# accepts every flag build-loop.sh passes.
INSTALL_ALIBUILD='alisw/alibuild@refs/pull/1038/head'
